<<~ kanawai "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119">>



<<~ ahu #/head>>

! The waking floor

''A vessel wakes at its MINIMUM available cap-stack, and gets RAISED by recognition.'' It never refuses to
stand because something it wants stands absent; it stands at whatever floor it can reach, says what it
cannot do, and waits for a raise. ''The raise'' names the transition.

''Capability arrives by recognition; it never gates on absence.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-problem>>

!! The problem the shape answers ~ a seal that eats a power cut

An at-rest seal exists against a ''STOLEN DISK''. A vessel that REFUSES TO BOOT without its passphrase turns
that seal into something else: an ordinary 3am power cut, with nobody awake to type, becomes an ''outage''.
For one operator's laptop that trade reads fine. For a crossroads a family depends on it reads indefensible
— ''the threat the seal guards against never arrived, and the seal took the place down anyway''.

The refusal also mis-names what happened. A vessel that cannot open its archive has not lost its ''identity''
— it has lost ''ACCESS TO SECRETS''. Those name different things, and only one of them should stop a node
standing.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-law>>

!! The law it re-uses ~ caps stack above a floor that never dissolves

The house already ruled this one plane over. `the-veil-ladder#the-base-model`:

> ''Anon reads as the ground, not a tier'': every vessel — anon, known, operator-with-node-infra, admin alike
> — stands anon at its floor (its own veiled-key, its own local content, no group) and ''MUST always fall
> back there''. Caps and node infrastructure stack ABOVE the floor; they never dissolve it.

''So a locked vessel has lost its CAPS, not its FLOOR.'' The veil-ladder already forbids the refusal: falling
back to the floor reads as the rule, and a vessel that will not stand at all has fallen ''through'' it.

''ONE LAW, TWO OCCASIONS.'' The ladder names the floor a BURN falls to (a chosen shedding); this names the
floor a WAKE rises from (an unchosen absence). Same floor, opposite motions — the tell that the
floor reads as real structure rather than as a special case either occasion invented.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-shape>>

!! The shape ~ rest, announce, raise

<<~ has Wake Stand "at/the-minimum-reachable-cap-stack ~ never a refusal">>
<<~ has Wake Serve "what/needs-no-secret ~ the public shelf serves without opening anything">>
<<~ has Wake Hold "closed/every-sovereign-act ~ a locked vessel gives no sovereign signature">>
<<~ has Wake Announce "loudly/what-it-cannot-do signed/always ~ an unsigned announcement spoofs and targets">>
<<~ has Wake Stamp "every-read/as-of-my-last-sync ~ a bare answer from the floor opens split-brain">>
<<~ has Wake Raise "by/recognition on/a-nonce-THIS-vessel-emitted ~ never a pre-baked blob">>
<<~ has Wake Expire "the-raise/lapses-back-to-the-floor ~ nobody lowers it; it simply stops being renewed">>

''ANNOUNCING carries more weight than it looks, and MORE than the first draft granted it.'' A vessel
standing at the floor and quiet reads worse than one refusing, because a caller cannot tell a held act from
a performed one. But the announcement ''MUST SIGN'': unsigned, it becomes a spoofable degradation signal AND
a targeting beacon naming which vessel sits unraised (#the-breaks c).

''THE RAISE REUSES MACHINERY THE MESH ALREADY RUNS'' — ''STRUCK, and the correction stands at #the-raise-is-a-vessel-layer-act.'' This paragraph proposed a kahu quorum signing the raise against the pre-rotated epoch chain. The founding order refutes it: declared Handles standing on an already-raised lararium seat a cabal, so a raise waiting on a quorum could never found the first Nexus — the dependency closes in a circle, and a kahu holding shares would become the collector this meme elsewhere forbids outright. ''What the mesh already runs and the raise MAY reuse'' reads narrower: the nonce-against-my-own-epoch-head freshness, never the quorum's authority.
''The vessel supplies the freshness'': it emits a nonce bound to its own epoch head and whoever raises it
signs THAT, because a clockless vessel accepting pre-signed material accepts a bearer credential that replays
forever (#the-breaks b).

''THE CUSTODY DOOR OPENS THE VESSEL'S OWN KEY, AND THE CREDENTIAL TAKES ONE ROAD.'' A vessel stands holding no vessel key: it binds its socket, serves the public shelf, reads no private row and mints nothing. `lares vault unlock` reads a credential on a TTY and sends ONE custody frame on the owner-only socket; the frame meets the custody door ahead of every verb, so it never becomes an invocation and leaves no digest, receipt, outcome or log line. The door hands the credential to the one holder in node-main, which opens the key and the custody root; the standing composition then tears down whole and a fresh one stands on the same floor with what the key opens. `lares vault lock` drops the key and returns the vessel to the floor, and so does every restart: nothing an unlock did touches disk. A refusal names its reading (`no-slot-opens` · `tree-absent` · `tree-unreadable` · `attempts-spent`) and never the input; three failed unlocks spend a boot's attempts, and only a restart restores them. A composition that refuses above the floor leaves the floor standing.

''A SEALED ROW'S ACT RIDES A CUSTODY FRAME, NEVER A STORE VERB'' (operator ruling). A store verb's output rests in the daemon's document in the clear, and the digest of its arguments rests in a receipt, so a roster key, a pet-name or a handle-book snapshot on that road would leave its sealed row for an unsealed document. A command that reads or writes a sealed row sends one `act` frame on the owner-only socket instead: the custody door runs the act on the vessel's main thread, against the key a person already opened, and answers that one caller. ''One typed table bounds the acts'' (`packages/lararium-node/src/sealed-acts.ts`). A frame names an act of the table and that act's typed arguments, and nothing beside them; an act the table does not name refuses by name, and so does an argument a row does not name. No row takes a credential of the vessel, and none hands out a seed or anything that rebuilds one. The restore of a face's backup bundle rides two rows that take the bundle's body still sealed and the bundle's own key, which opens that one body and nothing the vessel holds (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore">>). A root comes in through that door and none goes out through it: a root's recovery split hands a person two shares that rebuild the root, so it runs in that person's own process alone and refuses beside a standing vessel. No row signs whatever it gets handed: the one row that signs as a persona root reads the image first and signs the seal ceremony's own two images alone, a charter roll and a crossing record. The door logs an act's name and how it ended; nothing an act frame carries or answers reaches a receipt, an outcome or a log. ''One key session opens a vessel's rows.'' Beside a standing vessel a command opens none of its own: an unlocked vessel answers the act with no second prompt, and a locked one refuses and names `lares vault unlock`. With no vessel standing, the command runs the same act of the same table in its own process and opens its own session at the first sealed row the act touches. ''The fleet mirror of a face's own names rides the same door.'' A pet-name and a declared Handle rest sealed on their vessel and ride that face's OWN `@persona` plane to that face's other vessels. ''One face, one plane'': a name of the face at one handle-index lands on the plane of the group that face's sealed anchors name, and on no other, so a vessel that holds several faces tells no face's fleet about another face. After the sealed act lands, the command sends one more act frame that names the face and which of its two names, and no name: `selves.carry` reads the name off its own sealed row and writes it onto the plane from the vessel's main thread, through the Repo that holds the plane's document, and `selves.clear` clears a name once its sealed row holds it no more. Neither act moves a plane past what the sealed row says: a carry refuses where the row holds no such name, a clear refuses while the row still holds it, and both refuse a face this vessel holds no root for, so a vessel that never held a name clears nobody's. `lares persona sync` carries with `fill`, which lands a name only where the plane never held one: a device cannot tell its own older name from a newer one another vessel set, so a sync replaces neither a name nor a clear, and a rename on the device replaces it. The write needs no vessel key and crosses no worker as a message; it rides the custody door all the same, since that door carries the one frame that leaves no receipt, and since a locked vessel shuts every fleet lane. `selves.read` answers each carried face as its own plane names it, to its one caller. No verb takes or answers a face's own name, and a mirror act leaves no outcome, receipt, argument digest or log line behind it. The daemon island mounts the worn face's plane, so that plane's own document syncs between the main thread's Repo and the island's, as the plane itself; nothing beside the plane's document carries a name across that port. A command with no vessel standing reaches no plane: the act refuses by name before it opens any row, and the name stays on its sealed row until `lares persona sync` carries it. A clear made while no plane answered stays on its own vessel, since a sync carries names and never an absence: the person clears the name again beside a standing vessel, and that clear reaches the plane. ''How far a verb's record travels'': the daemon's own document rests in the main thread's Repo and in the vessel's own daemon island. Among socket peers it leaves a vessel toward one class alone, a socket the gate sorts same-operator (a holder of admin on the daemon bag, or a device the pinned persona KEL delegated), which the share verdict full-syncs (`packages/lararium-node/src/self-slot-share.ts`). A sibling, a contracted carrier, a walker and a stranger never read it. So anything a store verb records reaches each of the operator's own delegated devices that dials the vessel, and no one beyond them.

''A BACKUP ASKS THE VESSEL'S ROUTE AGAIN, ON A FRAME OF ITS OWN.'' An unlocked vessel's door answers any process of its own user, and no act of the table hands out a root. A face's backup bundle rebuilds that face's root for whoever holds the bundle's route, so the cut rides a `backup` frame apart from the act table (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-cut">>). The frame carries the vessel's own route, the face's handle-index and the bundle key, and nothing beside them. The holder proves that the route opens the key it already holds before any row opens; a route that opens no slot spends one of the boot's unlock attempts and refuses `no-slot-opens`, and spent attempts refuse `attempts-spent`. The vessel answers the bundle's body sealed under the bundle key, so no row's plaintext leaves it, and the command binds the bundle's routes in its own process, so the vessel never reads them. The door logs how the cut ended, by name, and nothing of the route, the key, the rows or the body.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-arrival-page>>

!! The arrival page ~ the floor still answers the door (RULED, BUILT but the announcement)

''A lararium standing at its waking floor still serves its arrival page.'' Liveness ⊥ readiness: the house
answering at its own origin names one fact, the house able to open its archive names another, and the
first MUST NOT wait on the second. The arrival page needs no secret — it rides the public shelf
(#/the-shape, //Serve//) — so a floor that withholds it has refused for an absence this law forbids gating on.

''The first arrival trusts the lararium's own origin'', and the lararium's own Pronaos serves the kindle
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/pronaos#/the-first-arrival">>). A house unreachable while it
waits for a raise would push that first arrival onto some other origin, and whatever origin answers first
decides what a fresh device runs. The floor serving its own arrival page keeps the first trust at home.

At the floor the page kindles nothing that needs a secret the floor does not hold, and it announces nothing:
the floor's announcement speaks to peers, never to the arrival (#/the-announcement).

''Built.'' The Pronaos composes before the boot reads the archive or the face (`lararium-node/src/main.ts`), from
the public genesis and the prepared Web artifact alone, so a lararium at its floor serves its arrival page, worker,
seed, seed-named CAS members and its `/.well-known/lar` descriptor exactly as a raised hearth does.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-announcement>>

!! The announcement ~ signed, per Nexus, to peers alone (RULED, UNBUILT)

''The floor's PEERS verify its announcement.'' A stranger at the arrival page holds no relation to
verify it against and needs none: it reads the same page from a floor and from a raised hearth. So the
announcement rides the socket or the relay, ''once per carried Nexus'', and NEVER `/.well-known/lar` or any other
public surface. The floor's public bytes stay identical to a raised hearth's, and a stranger probing the house
learns nothing of its standing (#/the-breaks (c): an unsigned announcement targets; a public one targets too).

''It signs under the domain `waking-floor-announce`'', which mints into the domain registry only when the
announcement gets built, at the re-found.

''It signs with the floor bundle's signing key for that Nexus'': a key derived from the floor key, the Nexus and the announcement's own floor route, so a locked vessel signs it (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange">>). NEVER the vessel key in public: the vessel key also signs carry seals, so one public signature under it would correlate the place with every Nexus it carries. Each Nexus therefore reads an announcement it can verify and no other Nexus can link.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/prior-art>>

!! What survives contact ~ the field ran this, and inverted our word for it

''A research spirit gathered outside negentropy'' (Night-Watch, 2026-08-08). The shape survives; the
vocabulary does not.

!!! ⚠ THE FLOOR STANDS AS THE HARDENED STATE, NOT THE DEGRADED ONE

''Android Direct Boot ships this, and reads it the opposite way we did.'' A phone boots into
device-encrypted storage only; credential-encrypted storage opens on first unlock —
`ACTION_LOCKED_BOOT_COMPLETED` fires at the floor, `ACTION_USER_UNLOCKED` marks the raise, and
`isUserUnlocked()` lets a component read which state it stands in. Alarms, SMS and accessibility run at the
floor.

''The forensics industry treats BFU as the HARDENED state and AFU as the liability'' — and Google agreed
hard enough to ''force the floor'': Android auto-reboots after 72 hours without an unlock attempt,
specifically to drag devices back out of extraction range.

''So `degraded` misnames the safe state and biases an operator toward rushing the raise.'' ''The floor holds
the RESTING state; the raise carries the risk.'' Every reading below re-seats on that.

!!! Three more that validate the shape

* ''SPIRE node attestation'' restates //capability by recognition, never gated by absence// almost verbatim
  — a workload bootstraps holding no secret, and identity derives from what it does and where it runs.
* ''Kubelet TLS bootstrapping'' ships the two-stage ladder (a limited credential buys a CSR, an approver
  raises it, rotation re-runs it) — and core Kubernetes ''refuses to auto-approve serving certificates by
  design''. The raise stays a deliberate act there too.
* ''Vault confirms the failure and confirms nobody cured it.'' A sealed Vault serves unseal and status — ''a
  boolean, never a cap-stack''. Auto-unseal trades the quorum for a hard KMS lifecycle dependency, and
  recovery keys ''cannot unseal''. Issue #16419 asks for exactly our design and sits open, unanswered.

!!! ★ THE KAHU MUST NOT HOLD SHARES ★

''Tang gives the escrow-free shape better than a share-holding quorum does.'' Tang stays stateless, "never
sees a single client key", gains no identifying information, and blinds the exchange so only the client
unblinds; Clevis `sss` already composes k-of-n over tang/TPM pins.

> ''Model kahu as Tang-shaped EXCHANGE parties, never as HOLDERS of KEK shares.''

A kahu holding shares ''becomes the collector'' — which collides
head-on with the house's own ''bind by edge, never by seed'' law. The prior art and the standing ruling
point the same way — the strongest signal available here.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-breaks>>

!! ★ ONE FLOOR — a herm grows a house when someone comes to dwell ★

''There stands no second KIND of Herm.'' A crossroads nobody has raised and a hearth whose archive holds shut rest in the same state, and naming them apart freezes a difference that does not survive contact: ''anyone who can raise a vessel at the floor may raise it on arrival.'' On Elyncia this reads as the ordinary thing rather than the exception — a wayside marker gets a building raised over it and a hearth fire lit when somebody comes to dwell, and the marker never stood as a different kind of thing from the house. Gaia's own crossroads shrines grew the same way.

''The 2026 rhyme carries it plainest'': a tablet left on a shelf, doing nothing anyone would call infrastructure, becomes the household's sync-node the day somebody needs one — and stops serving as one when nobody does. Same stone, same shelf; what changed reads as ''who came''.

''SEATED ⊥ RAISED, and the pair lets both laws hold.'' The Herm's `personaSlotCeiling === 0` bars a ''SEATED'' persona root — a human's key RESTING on a crossroads, the one thing the class exists to prevent. A raise seats nothing: caps arrive ''WITH'' a recognised operator, ride that operator's own keys, and stop standing when nobody renews the recognition. ''The vessel's at-rest state never changes'', so a stolen disk still yields nothing of anybody's person. Storage stays barred; presence stays welcome.

''MAY-HOLD-A-FACE ⊥ HOLDS-ONE-NOW — and `standAs` keeps the ARCHIVE question ALONE.'' `standAs(asked, archiveOpens)` answers ONE thing: what this vessel can OPEN right now. Whether a FACE stands — whether an operator has landed a PersonaGroup here — reads separately, because folding the two would make a faceless place class-`herm`, where `personaSlotCeiling("herm") === 0` bars the very face that would lift it. A face lit names what a vessel HOLDS; the archive opening names what it can OPEN, and a place founded with no face yet holds a crossroads that a founding act lifts.

''So the scenario the mesh actually needs already fits'': an operator stands a Herm, joins it to a Nexus, and leaves. A recognised operator arriving later raises its caps — for a visit or for a season — and it ''always falls back to the floor'', because the raise expires rather than anyone taking it away.

''A REALM STANDS APART FROM A VESSEL, and the no-lease doctrine governs the first.'' `cabal-realm` rules that a member holds no grant that expires — they ''dwell'', the dwelling deposits a settled depth, and stopping leaves no deed to lapse; //a lease makes a substance-object you hold between exercises, and this stack refuses that shape//. That law governs a REALM: a virtual relationship-place constituted by the people maintaining it. It does NOT govern INFRASTRUCTURE. A vessel's caps form no relationship, and a marker fencing them makes no claim on anybody's standing — reading the realm law onto the vessel layer repeats the same layer error this meme already names once, one plane down.

''What DOES cross the layers is the caution.'' The realm doctrine's own unswept corner — //soft-state rewards whoever keeps feeding, NOT who legitimately holds the realm; a small hostile faction can out-maintain an apathetic majority// — transfers intact to any raise that renews by PRESENCE. At the infrastructure layer it reads: whoever keeps touching a vessel keeps its caps, and diligence confers no title. ''Any presence-renewal MUST answer that, or it hands a squatter tenure.'' The walker's carriage at a hearth answers it: presence earns a guest notice before reclaim, the quota bounds what presence can hold, and the hearth's roll ends what it stops renewing (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-walkers-carriage">>).

''AN EPOCH FENCES PER NEXUS, and one frontier gives the raise BOTH properties it needs.'' A raise binds the Nexus's lease epoch: ''supersession'' (a newer grant binds a higher epoch) and ''expiry'' (an old one goes stale when the frontier rolls past it), both driven by the Nexus's own events rather than by a duration anybody must agree on, with no clock anywhere (#/the-raise-is-a-vessel-layer-act, where the rolling window of markers stands STRUCK). A vessel standing in more than one Nexus holds more than one frontier, and ''an epoch means nothing outside the Nexus that rolls it''.

''AND BELONGING TO A NEXUS CARRIES TWO CONDITIONS, not one.'' A node belongs by ''contract'' — the signed carriage relation — AND by ''agreeing with the grammar vectors traded on that Nexus''. The second carries more than ceremony: a node that cannot read what a Nexus trades cannot carry it faithfully, cannot verify what it relays, and nothing it never parsed can bind it. ''Contract without grammar-agreement reads as a signature over an unread page.'' — the grammar half stands UNBUILT and unnamed in code; its meme names a source file that does not exist.

''AND THE RAISE ENDS BY SUPERSESSION, never by a duration.'' A first cut expressed the expiry as a wall-clock interval, which fails twice: two islands with different clocks disagree about whether a raise stands, and a vessel AT THE FLOOR holds no trustworthy wall time at all — precisely the vessel this law governs. The clockless model already rules the form: a raise carries a ''corm epoch'' (a fencing token, never a timer), the vessel holds its highest-admitted epoch, and refuses anything beneath it. A paused, forwarded or replayed raise ''self-revokes the instant a higher epoch lands'' — no broadcast, no clock. Deciding //stop waiting for a renewal// rides the separate LIVENESS wire (local, suspicion-accruing, never leaving its island as a timestamp anyone must agree on); acting on a wrong suspicion stays safe because the fence still holds. ''Fusing the two wires smuggles a global now back in.''

''AND THE FENCE CARRIES TWO CONDITIONS the raise cannot hold without.''

# ''THE RESOURCE FENCES, or the model reads as nothing.'' Every guarded surface holds its own
  ''highest-admitted epoch'' and refuses anything beneath it. Where a surface keeps no high-water
  mark, two holders act on it at once and the epoch decorates a grant that fences nobody — the raise
  reads sound and the door stands open. ''A model that fences at the grant and not at the door has
  moved the check somewhere nobody performs it.''
# ''SPLIT-BRAIN NAMES THE REAL HOLE, and the liveness wire cannot see it.'' Two minters issuing the
  same epoch defeats a high-water mark exactly, because neither exceeds the other. The suspicion wire
  ''never says concurrent'' — it accrues doubt about a peer and reports nothing about the ORDER of two
  events — so it cannot adjudicate here, and no hand may ask it to. ''Ask the causal stamp for
  concurrency; a rhythm cannot answer it.'' Either the causal stamp adjudicates with a deterministic
  tiebreak, or one minter per epoch stands enforced.

''ROUGH AND TIGHT NEVER TRADE PLACES.'' The epoch counter carries a TIGHT guarantee — higher-than-N,
exact, logical. A witness carries a ROUGH one — an interval with its own stated uncertainty, useful
for calibrating a rate and load-bearing for neither ordering nor revocation. Asking the rough layer
for a tight answer smuggles the global now back under a different name, and a raise stays generous
against a witness radius rather than tuned to it.

''What stays OPEN, the whole of the remaining design'': what ''recognition'' means here (which edge, presented how, verified against what), and whether a season-long raise differs from a visit by anything but its renewal interval. The house already rules the shape — the raise expires, nobody lowers it, the operator seats a renewal interval rather than a rate — so the open part reads as ''whom the house recognises'', never ''how does it end''.

!! ★ THE RAISE STANDS AS A VESSEL-LAYER ACT — the layer error, named ★

''A kahu quorum CANNOT raise a vessel.'' The founding order refutes it outright: the cabal's declared Handles seat a kahu quorum, and they stand on an already-raised lararium, which stands on an operator opening that vessel's own archive. ''A raise that waited on a quorum could never found the FIRST Nexus'' — the dependency closes in a circle.

''So the layers separate, and each names its own actor'':

| layer | the act | who holds it |
| ① VESSEL | open this vessel's archive — floor → hearth | whoever holds THIS vessel's opening secret |
| ② PERSONAGROUP | stand a multitude; declare a Handle | the operator of a raised lararium |
| ③ KAHU-CABAL | the antigen · carriage contracts · the seal lineage | a quorum seated from declared Handles |
| ④ NEXUS | admit operators as kahu, or as contracted independents | the cabal that constitutes it |

''A quorum's acts sit at ③ and reach NO vessel's archive.'' Reading them into ① makes a kahu a share-holder, and a share-holder becomes the collector — the same law this meme already carries as ★ THE KAHU MUST NOT HOLD SHARES ★. The escrow-free shape (Tang: stateless, holding nothing) belongs at ① with a different holder, never at ③.

!!! ★ AND THE MECHANISM MATCHES THE ONE THE MESH ALREADY ROLLS — a lease, not a third invention ★

''A raise ENDS by non-renewal, exactly as every other grant in this house does.'' `raiseStands` reads `boundEpoch >= effective` against the Nexus's own lease epoch — the same comparison, against the same fold, that `device-delegation` already makes. The Nexus serves as the resource; its lease epoch, as the fence.

''Supersession and expiry come from that one mechanism, with no clock anywhere.'' A newer grant binds a higher epoch; an old one goes stale when the frontier rolls past it. A paused, forwarded or replayed raise self-revokes at the next roll — no global broadcast, no duration anybody must agree on, and a returning holder stays fenced whether or not anyone suspected it. This matters most for the vessel this law governs: ''a vessel AT THE FLOOR holds no trustworthy wall time at all''.

''A rolling WINDOW of markers stands STRUCK.'' A set answers //is this listed// and never //is this newer//, so a partitioned island could not reason about it locally; whatever fell off its far end encoded a retention policy nobody wrote; and a list of valid markers makes a roster of moments, which this house does not hold. The full reasoning, and the ownership cut that separates our fence from Keyhive's CGKA epoch and POSIX's time origin, stands at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/epoch-binding-surfaces">>.

''The ending rides a SECOND wire, deliberately unfused.'' Deciding //stop waiting for a renewal// cannot come from safety alone — under asynchrony nothing tells a slow recogniser from a departed one. That belongs to the liveness wire: local, monotone, suspicion-accruing, never leaving its island as a timestamp anyone else must agree on. Fusing the two smuggles a global now back in.

''The unattended crossroads therefore stays a ① problem.'' A vessel whose holder sleeps needs a way to open its OWN archive without a human — not a Nexus voting on it.

!! ⚠ Six breaks ~ what the proposal got wrong

''(a) WE DESIGNED THE RAISE AND NEVER THE LOWERING.'' The biggest hole. A raised vessel that stays raised
forever turns the floor into a one-time inconvenience and hands an adversary a fleet of permanently-raised
boxes — which is precisely why Android added its 72-hour forced return. ''The raise MUST expire back to the
floor on idle.''

''AND THE HOUSE ALREADY RULED THE CURE, one plane over.'' The identity plane holds that a standing lift
''EXPIRES rather than decays'', that ''no party exercises a lowering act'', and that the operator seats a
''renewal interval'' rather than a rate. ''Third occasion, same law'': the raise expires, nobody lowers it,
and a vessel nobody recognises any longer simply falls back to its floor.

''(b) "k-of-n material PRESENTED to it" describes a BEARER CREDENTIAL.'' A vessel at the floor holds no
clock and no trustworthy wall time, so signed material presented to it ''replays forever'' — a thief with
the disk AND one captured raise-packet raises the stolen vessel, and the seal-against-a-stolen-disk
evaporates. ''The cure needs verifier-chosen freshness'': the vessel EMITS a nonce bound to its own epoch
head, and the kahu sign ''that''. ''Never a pre-baked blob.''

''(c) THE ANNOUNCEMENT, UNSIGNED, ARMS TWO ATTACKS.'' It becomes a spoofable degradation signal (peers route
away from a healthy vessel) — and under the standing brief ''shelter against empire'' it becomes a
''TARGETING BEACON'', naming which vessel sits unraised, right now, and where. ''The floor's announcement
MUST sign'', and the roster of what-it-cannot-do reads as ''hostile-readable''.

''(d) A FLOOR READ WITHOUT A STALENESS STAMP OPENS SPLIT-BRAIN.'' GitHub's 2012 partition and GitLab's
Postgres split-brain both landed the same lesson: degraded-but-serving produced ~24 hours of hand
reconciliation, ''worse than a clean stop''. Every floor read MUST carry //as of my last sync// — which the
causal-islands law already demands and this surface had not yet honoured.

''(e) THE SHELF DISCIPLINE STANDS UNWRITTEN.'' Android's own doc names it: never place private user
information, passwords, or authorization tokens in device-encrypted storage. ''Our public shelf needs that as
explicit law with a review gate'', never as an intention.

''(f) "DEGRADED" MISNAMES THE SAFE STATE'' (#/prior-art). The floor rests; the raise risks.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-open-question>>

!! The downgrade attack ~ forcing costs nothing; the profit splits four ways

''An adversary CAN force the floor'' — cut the power. Cheap, deniable, repeatable. What that buys them
splits, and only the first line reads well for us:

<<~ has Profit Secret-harvest "verdict/NO ~ forcing the floor produces the BFU result extraction vendors call the BAD outcome. The floor holds.">>
<<~ has Profit Veto "verdict/YES-and-severe ~ a permanent deniable veto to whoever can drop power (MITRE T1562.010)">>
<<~ has Profit Raise-oracle "verdict/YES ~ forced reboots MANUFACTURE the moment kahu present material; repetition harvests it">>
<<~ has Profit Gray-failure "verdict/YES ~ peers cannot tell AT-THE-FLOOR from COMPROMISED-AND-IMITATING-THE-FLOOR">>

''THE VETO READS AS THE WORST OF THE FOUR, and it reads worst precisely because it stays QUIET.'' A civic
mesh whose function runs on sovereign acts hands a deniable, repeatable veto to whoever can reliably drop
power — and the result reads not as an outage (loud, escalatable, politically legible) but as ''silent capability
suppression''. So the floor MUST make suppression loud: ''an unexplained reboot deserves treatment as a
signal to peers, never as routine''.

''THE GRAY FAILURE names why the announcement must sign.'' Huang et al.'s differential observability: peers
cannot distinguish a vessel at the floor from one compromised and imitating it, and standard availability
mechanisms do not model that failure at all. ''Sign the state, or peers will trust an attacker's claim about
it.''

!!! What the field could NOT answer, which raises rather than lowers its priority

* ''No accepted freshness standard for presenting quorum material to a CLOCKLESS OFFLINE node.'' Break (b)
  sits on genuinely unmapped ground — the cure reads sound and nobody has standardised it.
* ''No security analysis of boot-to-floor as a named server pattern'', and no established name for it. The
  nearest handles read ''Direct Boot / BFU→AFU'' (shipped), ''progressive trust'' (Allen), and ''node
  attestation''. This carrier names it THE WAKING FLOOR.
* ''No field reports on NBDE or TPM at Pi-class civic scale'', and no published post-mortem uses "unseal
  storm" — mass-reboot pain surfaces only as feature requests and support threads.

!!! Where the field genuinely SPLITS, so no one mistakes a choice for a consensus

''Fail-secure vs fail-open carries no settled answer.'' Vault chose fail-secure and generated exactly our
complaint; GitHub's post-mortem concluded //fail-stop is dangerous… sometimes keep serving stale data beats
going silent//; Android chose fail-secure AND added a timer to force more of it. ''Anyone claiming this
settled sells one.'' Auto-unseal splits the same way — HashiCorp ships it as the operational answer,
practitioners argue it forfeits the point.

!!! The paths that do NOT carry a civic mechanism

* ''Tang/NBDE relocates the 3am outage rather than curing it'': an unreachable Tang server leaves a node
  retrying indefinitely rather than booting, and Red Hat's DR guidance demands redundant power AND network.
* ''The TPM path breaks on ordinary maintenance'' — PCR7 binding fails on firmware/kernel/bootloader update,
  and `systemd-cryptenroll` refuses to re-enroll with the same PCR parameters.
* ''Pi-class hardware carries no complete chain of trust'' without extra parts, and ''systemd-creds kills
  fleet provisioning outright'' — no machine but the local one can prepare the credentials.
  TPM and systemd-creds serve as ''local convenience'', never as
  the civic mechanism.

!!! What stays RULED, which none of the above re-litigates

The KEK stays ''passphrase-primary''; a random key beside the ciphertext reads as obfuscation posing as
isolation and gets refused; a missing passphrase ''NEVER silently degrades to weak key handling''. ''The
floor names WHAT A VESSEL SERVES, never what it weakens.''

''RULED'': every boot rests at the floor until a human unlocks, and nothing stored may lift it. An
unattended boot rests and never refuses.

''What the code does'': every boot composes at the floor. `packages/lararium-node/src/main.ts` hands its
first composition no sovereign readers, and a composition reaches a private row only through the custody
seat (`packages/lararium-node/src/custody-seat.ts`), which refuses `CustodyLocked` while no key rests in
it. A human's unlock raises the vessel. The keyhive archive, each face's veil archive and each face's
anchors rest sealed under the vessel key from the founding's first write, so no stored passphrase and no
unsealed archive lifts a boot.

''OWED'': a bounded ember, held as design input and unbuilt. It raises to SERVING alone and never
signs or admits; a count dial bounds it, and no clock; it wants the device's own unlock plus a second
signal; each raise gets recorded; any other device of the human removes it.

''OWED in code'': the passphrase lifecycle (`LARES_ARCHIVE_PASSPHRASE`) still seals the share carriers
(the device recovery shares and the reserve share) and lifts no boot; it leaves when those rows move
under the vessel key
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/the-road">>).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/elyncia>>

!! The Elyncia reading ~ the myth already carries it

''The fiction resolves the same tension it always described.'' After the Second Breaking, the gods bound
guardian spirits into orichalcum at surviving ley-line nodes. A shrine ''stabilizes through use'' — fed with
food, drink, incense, coin — and ''a fed node hums, a neglected one flickers''. The myth never said a
neglected shrine ''vanishes''. It flickers: present, diminished, waiting to be fed.

''A vessel waking at its floor stands as a flickering shrine.'' It stands at its crossroads, offers what it can
without its keeper, and brightens when someone comes to feed it. The reciprocal bond runs exactly as the
Lares always ran it — ''feed the Lar and it prospers you; neglect it and it turns its back'' — and turning
its back never meant leaving the crossroads.

''AND THE PLACE-BINDING SETTLES WHICH THING SURVIVES.'' A Lar binds to a PLACE, never to a family: if the
family moved, the Lar stayed. So a vessel that loses its operator's secrets loses ''the family's'' hold and
keeps ''the place'' — which is precisely the floor. The myth bore this load before the code needed it.

<<~/ahu>>

